import definition         stdlib/calculus/tends-to

theorem an-instance-spelling-the-bound-letter
  note        Inside the block letting ε, a line saying "for all ε" is put at ε/2, which spells the letter it binds.
  ε ranges over ℝ
  n ranges over ℕ
  let x : ℕ → ℝ                                                       (H1)
  let A ∈ ℝ                                                           (H2)
  assume x(n) → A as n → ∞                                            (H3)
  then For all ε > 0, there exists N ∈ ℕ such that |x(n) − A| < ε/2 for all n ≥ N.

1.  For all ε > 0, there exists N ∈ ℕ such that |x(n) − A| < ε/2 for all n ≥ N.
    proof
    let ε ∈ ℝ                                                         (K1)
    assume ε > 0                                                      (A1)

    1.1.  ε/2 > 0
          inequalities
            ε > 0            A1
            ε/2 > 0          multiplying by 1/2

    1.2.  For all ε > 0, there exists N ∈ ℕ such that |x(n) − A| < ε for all n ≥ N.
          def:tends-to L := A, from H3

    1.3.  There exists N ∈ ℕ such that |x(n) − A| < ε/2 for all n ≥ N.
          instantiate ε := ε/2 in line 1.2, from 1.1
